# Const-correctness

Mark an **instance** method `const` when it does not change logical object state. Refresh helpers that only re-apply strings or polish child widgets can be `const`.

Do **not** mark methods `const` when they change interaction state: which page is shown, session data, overlays, focus, or enabled actions. Calling non-const methods through a pointer member (`stack_->setCurrentWidget(...)`) is legal in a `const` method in C++ and is still a lie.

If the function never uses `this`, do **not** add `const`. Make it `static` or a free function in the anonymous namespace. `static` and `const` (on the method) are mutually exclusive.

```cpp
// BAD — static has no this; trailing const will not compile
static QSize probeVideoDisplaySize(const QString& path) const;

// BAD — const lies; the stacked page is observable state
void showWelcomeWindow() const { stack_->setCurrentWidget(welcomeWindow_); }

// GOOD — no this: static; read-only inputs are const T&
static QSize probeVideoDisplaySize(const QString& path);
QString color() const { return colorHex_; }
void showWorkWindowWithSetup(const QString& filePath, const QStringList& sourceVideoPaths);
void showWelcomeWindow();  // switches the visible page
void onVideoClosed();      // slot; clears session and navigates
```

Pass read-only objects as `const T&` (`QString`, `QSize`, structs). Pass cheap scalars by value. Prefer `const` locals.

Identity lookups on `QHash`/`QMap` (`EventCodeMap`) must not use `.value(key)` and treat a default `QString()` as a miss. Return `std::optional<T>` or branch on `constFind` (see `lookup-presence.mdc`).

Lambdas: capture only what you need. Capture `const T&` (or by value for cheap/const data) for reads; `&` only for locals the lambda mutates.

Use `mutable` only for caches/reentrancy guards.
